@bank all
// Pillar 5 (head writer, seat W): head-scratcher texts (seat H's case engine, 24k) and the Chief's notes. TEXTS.
// {machine} the machine's name, {shows} its card, {part} a part.

== case.tab.notes
- Fault notes
== case.tab.chief
- The Chief's notes
== case.title
- Head-scratcher: {machine} {shows}.
== case.suspects
- Suspects
== case.notes
- Notes
== case.cross
- Cross off
== case.uncross
- Put back
== case.call
- Call it: {part}?
== case.where
- Where next?
== case.hint.p3.1
- Have you read every note? One's on the machine.
// case.solved: shown only AFTER the fix works (the machine is right again), never at "Call it", so it can't give the answer away
== case.solved
- Fixed. That was it.
== case.wrong
- Not that one. Have another look at the notes.
== case.new
- A new head-scratcher is on the board.

// The Chief's notes, page 1: "What's been tried" (three lines, Calder's hand)
== chief.p3.title
- What's been tried
== chief.p3.1
- If someone fitted it and it's still wrong, it isn't that.
== chief.p3.2
- Read every note. They're not all on the card. Check the machine.
== chief.p3.3
- The scanner sees what's worn. The notes see what's been tried.

// ---- Pillar 5 Step 5 (SW3, 2026-10-26; S5-6, 24k's slot; spec section 10): head-scratchers P4, P5, P9 and the
// old-mysteries board. Placeholders as above, plus {thing} a gauge's name ("the panel gauge"), {word} a reading word
// ("high", "normal", "low"), {name} a person, and in P9 {thing} what they do ("the laundry wash"), {time} an hour, {when} as P1's.

// P4 Which gauge is lying? (from Day 17, Torres's word, needs the scanner): two gauges disagree; one of them is wrong
== works.mech.p4
- Which gauge is lying?
== next.p4
- Coming up: Which gauge is lying? Torres has one for you.
== next.wait.p4
- Which gauge is lying? Torres is waiting with a pair that disagree.
== case.title.p4
- {machine}: two gauges disagree.
== case.p4.head
- What each one says
== case.p4.gauge
- {thing} reads {word}
== case.p4.scan
- Your scanner reads {word}
== case.p4.call
- Call it: {thing} is lying?
== case.p4.right
- Fixed. New gauge in. Now they agree.
== case.wrong.p4
- Replaced {thing}. They still disagree. The other one was lying.
== case.hint.p4.1
- A third reading settles it. What does your scanner say?
== chief.p4.title
- Two gauges
== chief.p4.1
- Two gauges, two answers. Get a third. The odd one out is lying.
== chief.p4.2
- A gauge that reads the same all day is stuck, not steady.

// P5 Follow the line (M9's later stages; its power stage needs the conduit tester, so it waits for M19)
== works.mech.p5
- Follow the line
== next.p5
- Coming up: Follow the line. A fault somewhere along a whole line.
== next.wait.p5
- Follow the line: the Chief has a long one for you.
== case.title.p5
- {machine} {shows}. The fault is somewhere up the line.
== case.p5.stop
- {machine}: {word}
== case.p5.power
- A power line. Test each length with the conduit tester.
== case.p5.notester
- This one's a power line. You'll need the conduit tester first.
== case.hint.p5.1
- Start in the middle. Fine there? The fault is further on.
== case.wrong.p5
- New {part} in at {machine}. The line still {shows}. It's further along.
== chief.p5.title
- Long lines
== chief.p5.1
- Halve it. Then halve it again. Five tests find one fault in thirty.
== chief.p5.2
- Power's the same as water. The reading drops where the trouble is.

// P9 The clash: a fault only when two routines overlap; its fix moves a crew routine (or, with shutdowns, an hour)
== works.mech.p9
- The clash
== next.p9
- Coming up: The clash. A fault that only happens when two things do.
== next.wait.p9
- The clash: the Chief has a fault that won't happen on its own.
== case.title.p9
- {machine} {shows}, but only {when}. Nothing's worn.
== case.p9.head
- Who's using it then
== case.p9.who
- {name}: {thing}, {time}
== case.p9.move
- Ask {name} to move {thing} to {time}?
== case.p9.moved
- {name} moves {thing} to {time}. Nothing broke. Nothing needed fixing.
== case.p9.shut
- Your shutdown hour clashes with {name}'s {thing}. Move one of them.
== case.hint.p9.1
- Every part is fine. So what else happens at that hour?
== case.wrong.p9
- New {part} in. Same hour tomorrow, same trouble. It isn't a part.
== chief.p9.title
- Clashes
== chief.p9.1
- Some faults aren't parts. They're two good things at the same time.
== chief.p9.2
- The fix is a conversation. Ask nicely. Offer them a better hour.

// ---- the old-mysteries board (from Sunday Day 21, the Works bay beside the job board; five cases, order by schedU 854)
== mys.board
- Old mysteries
== mys.board.tap
- Old cards, pinned years ago, some in Calder's hand. Nobody's cracked them.
== mys.board.none
- You've taken them all. The board is bare. Torres says that's a first.
== mys.take
- Take this one
== mys.taken
- Taken. It's in your Fault notes, under the Chief's.
== mys.solved
- Solved. The card comes off the board. Calder adds a line to her notes.
== mys.rep
- Works reputation up: an old one cracked.
== mys.chief
- Old mystery cracked: {title}. Solved by {me}, Day {n}.
== mys.1.title
- The Tuesday hum
== mys.1.line
- A hum in the Deck 3 pipes, every Tuesday at four. Only Tuesdays. Seven years.
== mys.2.title
- The cold cabin
== mys.2.line
- One cabin on Deck 4 is always two degrees colder. Three crews have tried.
== mys.3.title
- The lift that waits
== mys.3.line
- The lift stops on Deck 3 by itself, late at night, and opens. Nobody's there.
== mys.4.title
- Calder's clock
== mys.4.line
- The bay's wall clock loses a minute a week. New clock, same minute.
== mys.5.title
- The garden flicker
== mys.5.line
- The garden lights dip for one second at dawn. Every dawn. Plants don't mind. Calder does.
== works.mech.mys
- The old-mysteries board
== next.mys
- Coming up: the old-mysteries board, in the Works bay. Old cases for a quiet day.
== next.wait.mys
- The old-mysteries board is up, beside the job board. Take one when you like.
